import GX.Spherical.ConeSurface

/-!
# The football: a cone sphere with two cone points of equal angle `θ`

Target `T-gx-cone-football` (`roadmap/roadmap.toml`), the non-vacuity witness asked by
`T-gx-spherical-cone` AUDIT-1 for `GX.IsConeSphere`. `papers/gx`, 0 `sorry`:

* `cex:start_singular_manifold_homeomorphic` — `RamcovGX.tex:1930` — partial, **not marked**:
  the football has `s = 2 < 3` cone points and is no instance of the cex (the row stays partial
  until `T-gx-troyanov`). It shows that the cex's objects, "a singular `𝕊²`-manifold homeomorphic
  to a sphere with `s` conical singularities of respective angles `θ_i`" (`GX.IsConeSphere`),
  exist with genuine cone points, for every angle θ ≠ 2π (at θ = 2π both poles are regular, (b)).
* **The football `F_θ`**, for every `θ > 0`: `GX.Football θ`, the round sphere `𝕊²` as a space
  (`GX.Football.homeoSph2`), with the maximal a.e. `(SO(3), 𝕊²)`-atlas
  (`GX.footballSingularGXStructure`) generated by `GX.footballAtlas`: the charts of the spherical
  cone `C_θ` (`GX.SphCone`) read through the open embedding `GX.footballEmb θ : C_θ → 𝕊² ∖ {S}`
  (`r e^{2πiφ} ↦ stereoInv(r e^{2πiφ})`, the apex to the north pole), and their transforms by the
  half-turn `GX.halfTurn ∈ SO(3)`, `(t, w) ↦ (−t, w̄)`, which reads `v ↦ 1/v` in the
  stereographic chart (`GX.halfTurn_smul_stereoInv`) and exchanges the poles. It is an a.e. atlas
  (`GX.isAEAtlas_footballAtlas`): across the two copies of `C_θ` the chart changes are read by the
  developing map of `C_θ` as the half-turn (`GX.footballAtlas_cross`,
  `GX.halfTurn_smul_sphConeDev`), and the support contains `𝕊² ∖ {N, S}`, portly.
* **The theorem**: `GX.isConeSphere_football` — `F_θ` is a cone sphere whose cone points are `N`
  and `S`, both of angle `θ` (`IsConeSphere (Football θ) ![θ, θ] ![N, S]`); `GX.football_sing_eq`
  (`sing F_θ = {N, S}` for `θ ≠ 2π`: exactly two genuine cone points), `GX.football_sing_eq_empty`
  (`F_{2π}` is regular; `N`, `S` are marked regular points), and **`F_{2π}` is the round sphere**:
  the identity of `𝕊²` is a chart of its atlas (`GX.football_two_pi_round`), an isomorphism onto
  the regular `(SO(3), 𝕊²)`-manifold `𝕊²` (`GX.football_two_pi_isAEMorphism_round`);
  `GX.exists_isConeSphere_two` (for
  every `θ > 0`, a singular `(SO(3), 𝕊²)`-manifold — locally connected, Hausdorff, second
  countable — homeomorphic to `𝕊²` with two cone points of angle `θ`, its singular locus when
  `θ ≠ 2π`); `GX.isSphericalConeSurface_football`.
* The structure maps: `GX.isAEMorphism_footballEmb`, `GX.isAEMorphism_footballFlip_comp` (the two
  copies of `C_θ`); **the half-turn is an automorphism** exchanging the cone points
  (`GX.footballFlip_mem_singularAut`).
* Tools of general use: `GX.isAEMorphismOf_maximalAEAtlas_of_forall` (a map reading the charts of
  a generating atlas as charts of another is an a.e. morphism of the maximal atlases),
  `GX.XAChart.ofIsOpenEmbedding` (an open embedding of a connected model which is an a.e.
  morphism is an `X_α`-chart, `def:atlas`), `GX.southPole`, `GX.range_stereoInv`,
  `GX.eq_southPole_of_fst`.

> Start from $\Sigma$ a singular $\SS^2$-manifold homeomorphic to a sphere with $s\geq 3$
> conical singularities of respective angles $(\theta_i)_{i\in \lsem 1,s\rsem}$. (l.1930)
>
> Endow $\widehat M$ with a Riemannian metric
> $$\widehat{\mathbf{g}}(\theta,r) = \mathbf{d r}\otimes \mathbf{d r}+
> \sin(r)^2 \mathbf{g}(\theta)$$
> which is "regular", eg locally isometric to $\SS^{n+1}$, on rays through "regular" points of
> $M$. (l.700)
>
> As a consequence, except for very particular $\Sigma$, $\Aut(M)$ is trivial. (l.1933)

## Hypothesis checklist

| paper notion | here |
|---|---|
| "singular `𝕊²`-manifold" (l.1930) | ✓ the library's `SingularGXStructure SO3 Sph2`, a maximal a.e. atlas (`cor:maximal_atlas`); `F_θ` locally connected, Hausdorff, second countable |
| "homeomorphic to a sphere" | ✓ `F_θ` is `𝕊²` as a space |
| "conical singularities of respective angles `θ_i`" | ✓ `GX.IsConePoint θ` at `N` and at `S` (the library's reading, `GX.Spherical.ConeSurface` SCOPE (a)), through `X_θ`-charts onto all of `C_θ` |
| `s ≥ 3`, Troyanov's condition | ✗ not an instance: `s = 2` (SCOPE (a)) |

## SCOPE (disclosed)

* **(a)** The football witnesses the cex's objects, not the cex: `s = 2`, the two angles are
  equal, Troyanov's condition fails (it forces three angles `≠ 2π`,
  `GX.TroyanovAngleCondition.three_le_card_ne_two_pi`), and `Aut(F_θ)` is not trivial (the
  half-turn, `footballFlip_mem_singularAut`), as l.1933 allows for very particular `Σ` — an analogy
  only: the `Σ` of l.1930–1933 have `s ≥ 3` and satisfy Troyanov's condition.
* **(b)** `θ = 2π`: the statement still holds, with no singular point — `N` and `S` are cone
  points of angle `2π`, i.e. marked regular points (`GX.IsConePoint.mem_reg`), and
  `sing F_{2π} = ∅` (`football_sing_eq_empty`); `F_{2π}` is the round sphere with two marked
  points (`football_two_pi_isAEMorphism_round`).
* **(c)** The metric is not formalized (as in `GX.Spherical.ConeSurface`). In the charts, the point
  of polar coordinates `(r, φ)` of the first copy of `C_θ` is developed at spherical distance
  `2 arctan r` from the north pole, and is the point `(1/r, −φ)` of the second copy, at distance
  `π − 2 arctan r` from its apex: the football is the Thurston suspension of the circle of length
  `θ` (l.697–700, `n = 1`) with `r ∈ [0, π]` and both ends collapsed, the two-centre reading of
  `GX.Spherical.S3Model` SCOPE (b). It is built by gluing two copies of `C_θ` along
  `(r, φ) ↦ (1/r, −φ)`, not through `GX.thurstonStructure`.
* **(d)** The planner's unchecked claim ([r], `roadmap.toml`) is checked: the chart at `S` is the
  apex chart after `(r, φ) ↦ (1/r, −φ)`, which is `v ↦ 1/v` in the stereographic chart, the
  half-turn `(t, w) ↦ (−t, w̄)` about the first equatorial axis, of determinant `1`
  (`GX.det_flipIsom`).

